"Exotic Dehn twists and homotopy coherent group actions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.11806)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Title: Exotic Dehn twists and homotopy coherent group actions ... We consider the question of extending a smooth homotopy coherent finite cyclic group action on the boundary of a smooth 4-manifold to its interior.\nTitle: Exotic Dehn twists and homotopy coherent group actions\nAuthors: Sungkyung Kang, JungHwan Park, Masaki Taniguchi\nDate: Wed Sep 18 08:45:47 2024\n\nWe consider the question of extending a smooth homotopy coherent finite cyclic group action on the boundary of a smooth 4-manifold to its interior. As a result, we prove that Dehn twists along any Seifert homology sphere, except the 3-sphere, on their simply connected positive-definite fillings are infinite order exotic.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHomological Nielsen realization for the manifolds $\\#_n \\mathbb{CP}^2$ (https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.27537)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Authors: Ethan Pesikoff ... Given a smooth, oriented, simply-connected $4$-manifold $M$, the homological Nielsen realization problem asks: when does a finite group of isometries $G\\leq O(H_2(M;\\mathbb{Z}))$ preserving the intersection form lift isomorphically to a finite group of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms?\nTitle: Homological Nielsen realization for the manifolds $\\#_n \\mathbb{CP}^2$\nAuthors: Ethan Pesikoff\nDate: Tue May 26 18:10:11 2026\n\nGiven a smooth, oriented, simply-connected $4$-manifold $M$, the homological Nielsen realization problem asks: when does a finite group of isometries $G\\leq O(H_2(M;\\mathbb{Z}))$ preserving the intersection form lift isomorphically to a finite group of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms? We study this question for the smooth, positive-definite 4-manifolds $M_n:=\\#_n\\mathbb{CP}^2$. Even though every isometry of $H_2(M_n;\\mathbb{Z})$ is induced by some orientation-preserving diffeomorphism, not necessarily of finite order, we show that Nielsen realization is sparse: as $n\\to\\infty$, a random subgroup of $O(H_2(M_n;\\mathbb{Z}))$ is asymptotically almost never realizable in $\\mathrm{Diff}^+(M_n)$; the same is true for random odd order elements of $O(H_2(M_n;\\mathbb{Z}))$. We present both positive realization results in certain cases and a range of obstructions to realization in other cases. The proofs combine equivariant connected-sum constructions, fixed-point theory for group actions on 4-manifolds, finite group actions on surfaces, analytic combinatorics, and previous work of Hambleton--Tanase.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Failure of Nielsen's theorem in higher dimensions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/30848087_Failure_of_Nielsen%27s_theorem_in_higher_dimensions)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Failure of Nielsen's theorem in higher dimensions ... Peer Reviewed http://deepblue.lib.umich.edu/bitstream/2027.42/47327/1/13_2005_Article_BF01220468.pdf ... In contrast, Raymond and Scott [30] showed that, in every dimension 3, there are nilmanifolds for which the Nielsen realization fails essentially using their nontrivial fundamental groups.\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# Failure of Nielsen's theorem in higher dimensions\n\n  * December 1977\n  * Archiv der Mathematik 29(1):643-654\n\nDOI:10.1007/BF01220468\n\nAuthors:\n\nFrank Raymond\n\n  * University of Michigan\n\nLeonard Scott\n\n  * University of Virginia\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nPeer Reviewed http://deepblue.lib.umich.edu/bitstream/2027.42/47327/1/13_2005_Article_BF01220468.pdf\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nContent uploaded by Frank Ray