n the boundary of a smooth 4-manifold to its interior. As a result,\nwe prove that Dehn twists along any Seifert homology sphere, except the 3-sphere,\non their simply connected positive-deﬁnite ﬁllings are inﬁnite order exotic.\n1 Introduction\nDehn twists are fundamental in the study of mapping class groups of surfaces. In\nthis article, we study their generalization in dimension 4. Let Y be a closed oriented\n3-manifold with a nontrivial element ϕ ∈π1(Diff+(Y)), based at the identity. Then ϕ\ninduces a self-diffeomorphism of Y × [0,1],\nΦ: Y × [0,1] →Y × [0,1];\n(s,t) ↦→\n(︁\nϕt(s),t\n)︁\n.\nGiven a smooth embedding of Y × [0,1] into a compact smooth 4-manifold X, the\nself-diffeomorphism Φ can be extended to a self-diffeomorphism of X. The resulting\ndiffeomorphism, tX : X →X, is called a 4-dimensional Dehn twist on X along Y.\nMoreover, if Y is the boundary of X, then we have a Dehn twist supported in a collar\nneighborhood of the boundary, and we call such a diffeomorphism a 4-dimensional\nboundary Dehn twist. While Dehn twists can be considered in any dimension, we will\nhenceforth omit the dimension, as this article focuses solely on 4-dimensional Dehn\ntwists.\n✉S. Kang\nsk2431@cam.ac.uk\nJ. Park\njungpark0817@kaist.ac.kr\nM. Taniguchi\ntaniguchi.masaki.7m@kyoto-u.ac.jp\n1\nDepartment of Pure Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics, University of Cambridge,\nCambridge, UK\n2\nDepartment of Mathematical Sciences, KAIST, Daejeon, Republic of Korea\n3\nDepartment of Mathematics, Kyoto University, Kyoto, Japan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nK-Theory 4: 29-53, 1990. (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=38bdcae48854e17e77f7756aa891a02944a8c26e&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; injectivity of a certain 'assembly map' from group (K-)homology to (localized) ... For a finite group G and a G-aspherical G-space 141, the ... Madsen-Rothenberg [23] for the odd-order